  "updated": "2026-09-03T01:08:59.960621+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSurface high pressure and upper level ridging continues the hot\nand humid conditons across our area through Friday. A cold \nfront will then pass through from north to south by late Friday \nand into Saturday morning. This high pressure system will have \ndry air infiltrate into the mid to upper levels levels according\nto forecast soundings. This will help out with suppressing much\nin the way of severe weather for Thursday to the southern half \nof our area. Hi-res models do not have much activity for \nThursday although the HRRR does have a complex coming through \nand just skirting our northern counties by 18Z. \n\nSome activity or thunderstorms are suggested by the HRRR for \nlater in the afternoon and possibly into early evening further \nsouth. This dry air may provide caps and suppress this solution.\nHowever, SPC does keep a slight risk for the northern half of \nthe area and a marginal threat for the southern half of the \narea. Any strong to severe storms will likely be isolated in \nnature. With the lack of sufficient shear this will justify the\nisolated nature. The main impacts would be damaging wind gusts \nand/or large hail.\n\nWPC does have us in a marginal threat for excessive rainfall \nfor Thursday for the northern half of the County Warning Area. \nThis implies that there could be isolated flash flooding \nalthough a low probability will exist. Especially since we have \nbeen dry, however the thing we have going for us is high PWATS \nwith sufficient DCAPE to allow for the potential. There will be\nenough instability, especially with the anomalously high \ntemperatures in place for Thursday, which would cause some \npotential for heavy downpours and lead to localized flooding \nissues although this would be confined to flood prone or poor \ndrainage areas. \n\nFor Friday, as the aforementioned cold front sags south through\nthe area we could have another round of strong to severe \nweather. SPC does have us in a marginal threat for entire area \nexcept for the southern portions of West Virgina which are \nunder a slight risk for severe thunderstorms. The main threats \nwill be damaging wind and/or large hail. WPC does have us under\na marginal threat for excessive rainfall for much of the area. \nLocalized flash flooding is possible, but has a low probability\ndue to dry antecedent conditions.\n\nThe heat advisories were left untouched as far as coverage goes\nalthough it was extended into Saturday until 00Z. For Friday, \ntemperatures are expected to be slightly lower although heat \nindex values will still be within advisory criteria and expected\nconvection may relinquish some of the heat index values later on\nin the afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nOnce the main feature passes through the area for Saturday, as \nmedium range models have slowed down the progress of frontal \npassage across the area, therefore we may see reprieve from \nsevere weather potential on Saturday. Chances of showers and/or\nstorms will still exist for the morning and afternoon, however \nthings will shape up by evening. \n\nMedium range models then suggest much drier conditions for \nSunday and into the new work week as high pressure builds back \ninto the area. Diurnal chances for shower or storm activity will\nlikely only be isolated in nature for Sunday through Tuesday \nand will mainly be confined along the mountains. Cooler and \ndrier weather returns although temperatures will remain just \nslightly above average for this time of year, however due to the\nprevious hot and humid conditons it will feel a lot more \ncomfortable.",
